[Music]  welcome back to our energized developer  Community here in Munich and to those of  you watching online I hope you grabbed a  coffee and stretched your legs so for  our first tech track today we're going  to hear some reasons why your Java app  should find its home on Google cloud and  I'd like to welcome application  development customer engineer for  startups and scale-ups Dario Banfi  [Music]  Google Cloud next here in Munich uh it's  so great to see so many of you  interested in Java especially since  there is also a goaling track going on  in the other room but it's good to see  so many Java developers  um  today I'm going to talk about five  reasons why your Java apps are better on  Google Cloud  and I know it's quite a bold statement  here I have 20 minutes to explain myself  so yeah I I'll get to it soon  my name is Dario Banfield I'm a customer  engineer here in Munich I actually live  100 meters from this venue and this is  not why I got invited to talk here but  the reason why I'm here it's because I'm  a Java developer myself  I've done Java on multiple layers of the  stack I've built a graphical user  applications with Java Swing  I recommend it I've used a lot of spring  I've done Android development kotlin  development so I've seen Java in a lot  of different forms and one thing I want  to talk about is how I think that Google  cloud is a great way to ask your job  application so let me let me get to it  let me get to the reason so reason  number one it's about being able to  deploy in seconds  so whenever you write an application you  don't want to spend a lot of time uh you  know dealing with servers jvm setting  your Java home you you want to just be  able to write your code and have it  running on Google Cloud very quickly  and with this you can do it very easily  with cloudrun on Google Cloud so Cloud  run is our fully managed container based  serverless platform  it lets you build your code package it  as a container and deploy it on top of  our scalable infrastructure and it gives  you a lot of features out of the box  like Auto scaling scaling to zero  observability and so on  so it's a great fit for Java and also  other languages of course but it's a  it's a great way for you to really save  on you know the deployment time that you  would otherwise be spending and the good  thing about Cloud run there was the quiz  question earlier it supports any  language you just need to package your  application as a container  and if you're using Java you don't even  need to write a container a Docker file  actually we support building from source  using uh under the HUD Cloud native  buildbacks with Builder packs you can  our software automatically detect the  language of your application and we'll  create a container for it so that you  don't even need to do that  let's let's see an example right so I  will walk you through a fictitious Java  application that we will create because  I don't have a demo but it's quite  simple so we can follow it like this  first of all we will curl from the  spring website the starter kit choosing  dependency web so this is just like a  simple Java API service that we want to  deploy  we don't load it on our local machine  and after that we unzip it and then we  will open the demo application Java so  the main class where our application  code will run  once we do that we just want to add a  sign of life right like a message from  saying hello from cloud run so in Spring  we just put an annotation a rest  controller we put a get mapping with a  root URL and then we are good to go  our simple application is ready we can  deploy it we can test it out  and when it comes to the deployment  phase I I'm a lazy developer and I  always ask myself what is the least  infrastructure management that meets my  needs  and I already said Cloud run is actually  a very good use case for this so we can  use cloud runs we go in the directory of  our code and all we need to do is run  the command gcloud run deploy  this command will interactively ask us  which apis we want to enable for example  artifact registry right like we want  this container stored in a container  registry and then it will ask us which  region we want to deploy it into  as of now we have 35 regions and just at  this conference we are announcing so  many more six or five so  um you have like quite a lot of choice  in order to get your applications very  close to your users  if you do need more customization you  can also put a cloud builder.tml file in  your directory this will gives you the  configuration needed to for cloud build  our CI CD manage the platform and  for example is never a bad idea writing  some tests before deploying the code so  you can do this with Cloud build you can  add multiple steps and in Cloud build  each steps will be run by a separate  container so that you can choose very  specifically which dependency you want  to have in your container in order to  deploy your application so when you  deploy for example we can use the  container Cloud SDK which is provided by  us and lets you have like a gcloud  command line configured and ready to go  all right so this was a reason number  one ease of deployment  now let's let me get to reason number  two  after you deploy your application it's a  good practice to not have the entirety  of your traffic going to the new version  right away in case you have some issue I  don't know testing doesn't always catch  the issue so this practice is called the  canary deployments Canary rollouts and  it normally takes quite some effort to  build it yourself right like you need to  implement this logic and have this as  part of your cicd which is quite some  work and I don't want to do that  so we can use a cloud run a traffic  splitting feature for that  and for those of you who have seen it  you can go in the console you will have  your button managed traffic and from  there you will be able to select how  much traffic you want to each revision  a good practice is for example deploying  the fourth container with no traffic and  then scale it up and you can do this in  multiple multiple ways you can do it in  the console you can do it with a command  line you can do it uh probably like the  best will be to script is as part of  your CI CD so that's it's an automated  process ideally like with also some  automated monitoring in case your  production issues as some bugs so the  possibility are endless but the cool  thing is that it's really part of cloud  run and it's very simple to use  all right let me get to reason number  three so and this is really Java focused  right so  a couple of months ago we announced that  we have some optimization with Java  client libraries  so if you use for example uh bigquery or  if you use Pub sub or if you use any  Cloud native Google cloud service in  your application you probably have a  library for that  and our libraries now support natively  code compilation  for uh for uh for a native executable in  Java so that's a quite a good thing that  takes you that so you don't have to do  it yourself right you don't have to play  around with the configuration which is  also quite some work  I'll explain a bit more about that but  let me zoom out a bit let's talk about  Cloud run right uh  Cloud run as I said gives you Auto  scaling out of the box so you have here  three containers right your application  is getting a lot of traffic so Cloud run  started multiple instances of the  service  what happens that if the traffic goes  down Cloud run will also scale down  until you get to zero no more containers  and this is normally quite good for you  because you're actually not paying for  this container right so scaling to zero  is actually a very useful feature  depending on the application you have  the problem is that whenever you will  get the first request you will have  what's called in cloud computing as a  cold start so cold start me are is the  delay that it takes from cloud run to  start your container from a state where  there is no containers  and your users can experience that and  of course it can be annoying or  depending on your application it can  become a problem  so how do we solve that there is  a couple of ways the simplest one is to  use in Cloud run the concept of minimum  instances  with minimum instances you say that I  want this amount of container to be  running always at the same time so that  you completely avoid the problem of cold  start the container will be always  running but you also lose the advantage  that we discussed that you are going to  be paying for this container actually  the price is a bit lower so if you check  on the pricing page you can learn about  it but still you're paying the right and  we don't want that  and the second way to do it is Java  native compilation  this became a really popular a couple of  years ago with ground VM I'm sure many  of you already play with that and the  difference is quite significant for  cloud native application especially for  containerized workloads that run  kubernetes or a serverless platform and  let's see an example right I I made with  the same service I downloaded earlier I  made an example between a native  application which will have a startup  startup time of around 486 millisecond  and your traditional Java application  where the startup time will be around  seven seconds so this is almost like one  order of magnitude more and yeah it's  it's quite relevant  native compilation has other benefits  also there is not only startup time it's  also about memory utilization Peak  Performance  and it also has just some drawbacks but  depending on your use case it can be a  great uh tool to use and let me quickly  explain the difference  um between just in time compilation  which we use in your traditional Java  application so you write your Java code  it gets compiled to bytecode and then  this bytecode is translated to machine  code Just In Time by the Java virtual  machine  when you use ahead of time compilation  this is done ahead of time this is done  before so the this the machine code will  be generated and will be run directly on  the architecture where you are deploying  to  um and uh yeah as I said for cloud  native workloads it can be really  Adventures advantages  so going back to the initial  announcement uh we all of our libraries  supported Java native complete  compilation and yeah I invite you to try  this out and in your application and I  I'm also going to give you a couple of  concrete tips on how to do that  um if you're using for you're using for  example spring native which is probably  like the most popular Java framework I  would say you can further improve the  performance by optimizing the classes  that get loaded in the class path as  startup time so for example we have a  spring accept expression language we  have XML if you don't need them you can  use this configuration to remove them  you can also use a spring native class  initialization so for example you can  say which classes should be initialized  at build time versus runtime and I could  take talk for hours here because Java  typically initializes classes at runtime  and this is as like a of course like a  latency  tall on your application  so with this Flex you can reduce that  it's not super simple right like you  need to read a bit which classes can be  initialized at real time because it's  not valid for all type of classes they  need to be safe classes but it's also  like another performance Improvement  that you can Leverage  and then lastly it's a native quick  build mode so if you ever built a Java  native application and you don't have a  powerful computer you know the pain it  will start flying out of your room  so with Native quick build mode you can  actually reduce the time it takes to  compile your Java application you just  pass the minus zero B flag  and the the the timer will be quite  significant so this is the time it takes  for a normal compilation right so 63  seconds four gigabyte of memory and we  take quick build mode it's going to be  42 seconds and less than half the memory  so it's pretty good especially if you  build hundreds of containers per day  all right let me go to number four it's  about security we talked already a lot  about that about uh you know salsa which  is not the Mexican Dipa but it stands  for supply chain levels for software  artifacts  it's a security framework that gives you  some prescriptive measures on how to  secure your cicd pipeline and this  became quite relevant after the  solarwinds heck a couple of years ago  where the cicd was compromised and some  malicious code was deployed to multiple  environments  so if you want to enable sign builds you  can use binary authorization this makes  sure that only code that gets built by  your CI CD pipeline is allowed to be  deployed in cloudron  let's see how it looks so if you go on  cloud run you can go to your services  and you can go on the details page in  the console for example  from there you will see that there is a  binary authorization flag so you can  enable it  and once you enable it you have to  configure a policy which which you can  do by clicking the button or scripting  it  once you do that you will see that there  is a summer testers  testers are entities which are allowed  to sign a given build and this build  will be cryptographically signed right  so in our policy we will click edit and  we will say that  only a specific a tester like built by  Cloud build in this case is Maya tester  will be allowed to deploy on cloud run  so you just click the checkbox required  attestation and from now on no other  image which is not built by Cloud build  will be allowed to be deployed so it's  pretty neat so you you know for sure  that no one can just deploy an image  from the docker registry without  approval and you can make sure that your  CI CD becomes the central place where  security is enforced for example you  create some Security checks you create  some building ability scanning we talked  about today about open source Library  scanning so this is a great place to do  it and it's a great way to enforce it  all right so this was my reason number  four so now comes to the last reason  which is actually an announcement we are  making at next  and the announcement is that Google  Cloud joined the eclipse adoption  working group  eclipse adoptum is a consortiums of java  leaders which is uh the entity behind  the temori build of java  has been become really popular in the  past years it's one of the mostly most  used jdk and it has like the highest  standards of uh security of  compatibility so from now on uh or like  not now on but very soon you will be  able to use the Tamarin build in all of  our serverless products so including for  example Cloud functions app engine so it  will be the jdk behind the your Java  application so we are looking forward  for you to try this out  and yeah so let me recap I covered a lot  of ground so five reasons easy  deployments and Payless with Cloud run  salsa compliant uh out of the box a lot  of features  one of these is of course Advanced  deployment patterns using a traffic  splitting using this concept of canary  rollouts  you get native image support for your  Java client libraries so  go on and try building your Java  applications to native code  and you get security from the CI CD  using signed bills to production using  very soon the tamarina jdk into your  application  all right so that was it I hope I could  teach you something new and I hope you  enjoy the rest of the session today  thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

